      "notes": [
        "**Ukrainian has no separate \"-ing\" form.** *Я читаю* means \"I read\" **and** \"I'm reading\" at once. There's no equivalent of English *am reading*. Good news: one form covers both, and context does the rest.",
        "**Every verb falls into one of two patterns.** The difference is a single letter in the third-person form: pattern one **читає** (-е-), pattern two **робить** (-и-). If you know that one form for a verb, the other five follow without memorizing.",
        "**Роблю, not робю.** Second-pattern verbs whose stem ends in **б, п, в, м, ф** slip in an **-л-** only in the *я* and *вони* forms: *роблю / роблять*, *люблю / люблять*, *сплю / сплять*. The four forms in between are unaffected. ⚠️ A small pattern, but it comes up constantly.",
        "**Нічого не роблю — two negatives in one sentence.** Ukrainian requires a double negative: *нічого* (nothing) **still** needs *не*. Same with *ніхто не знає* (nobody knows), *ніколи не був* (has never been). Drop either one and the sentence breaks.",
        "**Дивлюся телевізор, but дивлюся на нього.** The verb *дивитися* (to watch, to look) pairs with **на** when what you're looking at isn't a show: *дивлюся на тебе* (I'm looking at you). For TV and films, no preposition.",
        "**Сумувати за — to miss.** Literally \"to grieve behind\", and whatever you miss goes into the орудний: *сумую за домом*, *сумую за мамою*. You'll also hear *скучати*, but *сумувати* is more Ukrainian.",
        "**Що can mean \"what\" and \"that\".** *Що ти робиш?* (what are you doing) and *Він каже, що…* (he says that…). The only difference is position: at the start of a sentence it asks; in the middle, after a comma, it links."
      ],
